  "previous_finding": "P1-AUXILIARY-SCAN-LOCK-ORDER; canonical release-auxiliary-lock-independent-review-v1.json and private original report retain source 0d26e929 and original static proof.",
  "dispositions": [
    {
      "topic": "Owning capsule lock inversion",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED",
      "evidence": "task_store.py:510-556 releases every held state lock before waiting on the auxiliary identity lease, reacquires the original locks in deterministic UUID order, and compares each persisted aggregate disk generation. A change rejects staged authorization via ConflictError instead of continuing."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Global uniqueness and foreign validation",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED",
      "evidence": "auxiliary_stores.py:109 acquires the auxiliary identity lease before the unchanged exhaustive branch of _find. Lease persists until transaction cleanup. Foreign capsules are still loaded through canonical recovery, all domain models and event history validation. _ensure_upsert_owner and write-side identity/idempotency checks remain active. No lock-free state/journal reader or derived owner index introduced."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Foreign cached snapshots",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED_CODE_REVIEW_DIRECT_REGRESSION_ADDED_EXECUTION_PENDING",
      "evidence": "task_store.py:544-550 retains previously locked/staged owners, evicts unlocked clean foreign snapshots after the first lease acquisition, and rejects dirty foreign state lacking an update lock. Thus first global identity discovery reloads foreign state after waiting. Once the auxiliary lease is retained, normal auxiliary writers cannot advance that foreign auxiliary state before transaction completion. Newly reviewed test_identity_lease_refreshes_foreign_read_cache:522-539 preloads foreign B while A owns its update lock, lets another UoW commit the matching provider UUID to B, then verifies A cannot claim it using its stale cached B view."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Partial handoff failure",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED",
      "evidence": "task_store.py:551-555 marks the session invalid, releases partially reacquired state locks and the auxiliary lease. _ensure_open:1473-1479 blocks commit/pending_events and future global identity acquisitions until rollback. rollback:1432-1442 discards staged aggregates/project state, resets invalid status, releases remaining leases. Injected lease/reacquisition failures and stale generation tests allow successor UoWs to commit."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Same-thread nested transaction ownership",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED",
      "evidence": "task_store.py:523-526 rejects a different same-thread session already owning the auxiliary lease before releasing its state locks. Registry ownership is protected by the existing guard, installed after actual acquisition, removed only for the matching lease token in cleanup. Already-owned lease calls return idempotently at :518-519. This prevents FileLock thread reentrancy from admitting competing uncommitted nested identity claims."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Memory lock ordering",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED",
      "evidence": "unit_of_work.py:43 attaches _check_auxiliary_lock_order, which rejects first auxiliary acquisition while the original memory adapter retains its write lease. memory_store.py:267-270 exposes existing retained lock state without changing memory locking/validation/writes. Auxiliary-before-memory remains allowed; already-owned auxiliary lease never repeats the guard. Reviewed production auxiliary callers do not access uow.memory before identity operations."
    },
    {
      "topic": "New reservation",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED_CODE_REVIEW_NEW_ROLLBACK_ASSERTIONS_EXECUTION_PENDING",
      "evidence": "Reacquired genuinely new aggregates skip disk-generation comparison because no canonical generation exists; their original aggregate/reservation and reacquired state lock remain retained. The test:492-519 now covers both durable new project/run auxiliary identity creation and a second reserved run/claim abandoned via rollback, asserting neither run nor claim survives a fresh reader. Initial v2 execution predates those added rollback assertions."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Scoped reads",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED",
      "evidence": "ProviderRepository.get and ExecutionRepository.tool gain optional keyword run_id. Scoped _find resolves the owner document and validates embedded ownership/duplicates, returning None for missing owner/foreign identifier. AgentExecutionService initial/completion reads use their known run_id. ToolService initial provider lookup remains generic before owning run lock, then known tool lookup is scoped. Source search found no additional repository implementations/test doubles requiring signature adaptation."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Callsite and transaction lifetime",
      "disposition": "ACCEPTED_WITH_DECLARED_SERIALIZATION_COST",
      "evidence": "Provider/tool/review/delivery/recovery writes all route through the retained global identity lease. External model/tool execution occurs outside the corresponding committing UoW. Existing controller leases remain held through handoff. All state locks are dropped before releasing the auxiliary lease during cleanup; no new per-project mutex is added to every UoW."
    }
  ],
